         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgWebbDisc\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgWebbDisc', '0%')\" src=\"\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Disk of gas and dust around a young star (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c7877ca6-2d32-4999-b87d-7fd35429e58c",
                     "text": "ESA/Webb, NASA & CSA, M. Villenave et al.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### How Is a Planet Formed?\n\nThe image in this section shows a disk where a planet is forming. This region is known as **IRAS 04302+2247**. It lies 525 light-years from Earth, which is about 4.97 quadrillion kilometers (4,966,920,000,000,000 km) away (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c7877ca6-2d32-4999-b87d-7fd35429e58c",
                     "text": "ESA/Webb, NASA & CSA, M. Villenave et al.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "8",
                        "day": "29"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #D7FFE4;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\" style=\"background-color: #D7FFE4;\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-infinity\" style=\"font-size: 1em;\"></i> <i class=\"fa-solid fa-atom\" style=\"font-size: 1em;\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #D7FFE4;\"><b>Mathematics and Physics in Geography</b><br /><br />Have you ever stopped to think about what 525 light-years really means as a distance?<br /><br />A year is a unit of time, so first we need to see what a “year” means here.<br />A light-year is the distance light travels in one year. That is why, when we look at something 525 light-years away, we are actually looking into the past (<htcite0>): the light has been traveling for 525 years to reach us.<br /><br />Those photons never stopped along the way, so their trip lasted 525 years, and every second they covered the same distance. In other words, we add the same distance over and over, one year after another (<htcite1>).<br /><br />So to find the total, we only need to multiply the distance covered in ONE year by 525.<br /><br />What is that distance?<br /><br />Light travels at about 300,000 km per second.<br />Multiply that by the number of seconds in a year, and then multiply the result by 525:<br /><br />525 light-years = 525 × distance per year<br />525 light-years = 525 × (distance per second × seconds per year)<br />525 light-years = 525 × [(distance per second) × (seconds per day × days per year)]<br /><br />Putting the numbers in:<br />525 light-years = 525 × [300000 × (86400 × 365)] = <b>4,966,920,000,000,000 km</b></div></div></center>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4a1aea2e-aa0d-49b1-9e69-27743bd2c8e5",
                     "text": "ESA",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2004",
                        "month": "10",
                        "day": "09"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "09"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2004",
                     "month": "5",
                     "day": "19"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2025",
                     "month": "5",
                     "day": "9"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "This result is an approximation, and we will refine it as we study Earth further in the Atlas.\n\n#### We Are All Stardust\n\nPlanets form from clouds of dust, just like stars (<htcite0>).\n\nAt the beginning of this process, there are very few dust particles. As the cloud collapses, the dust grains move closer together and collide, forming larger clusters known as planetesimals (<htcite1>; <htcite2>; <htcite3>).\n\n#### Planet Growth\n\nThese planetesimals keep colliding with other particles and grow into an even larger structure called a protoplanet.\n\nOver time, the protoplanet gathers more and more material until it becomes a fully formed planet (<htcite4>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d4ed59c5-0332-4f5a-a662-dcf5549616ba",
                     "text": "NASA, Stars",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "28bbb4a2-2b1c-4704-ac30-110753f74d23",
                     "text": "Morfill, G., et al.",
                     "page": "pp. 212, 216 - 217",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1978",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "cfa22d73-c494-4b2d-b9d5-d19292c61b09",
                     "text": "Birnstiel, T., et al.",
                     "page": "pp. 42, 46 - 47",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2016",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "79d13e10-a273-420f-bbb6-61e06a499a85",
                     "text": "Sacani, Sérgio",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2017",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "722f0c13-990d-4452-bd95-6cc9f7031bf1",
                     "text": "Leinhardt, Zoe M., and Derek C. Richardson.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2005",
                        "month": "",
