                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### What Is a Planet?\n\nToday there is broad consensus on how planets form.\n\nHowever, the definition of a planet has been updated in recent years. To count as a planet, an object must have enough mass for its own gravity to pull it into a sphere, it must orbit a star, and it must clear other objects out of its path (<htcite0>; <htcite1>).\n\n#### Applying the Scientific Method\n\nWhat we know about how planets form is fairly recent. Before <htdate0>, people believed that planets could not form in the early stages of the universe.\n\nHowever, measurements from the Hubble Space Telescope showed that planets were already forming in the first phases of cosmic history.\n\nThis may have happened because the disks that planets grow from lasted longer in the early universe, giving them the time they needed to form (<htcite2>).\n\nThis discovery shows that in science, knowledge is always being updated and expanded.\n\n**In short:** planets grow from a cloud of dust around a young star. The grains stick together into planetesimals, the planetesimals crash together into a protoplanet, and in time the protoplanet becomes a full planet.",
